[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Collections Framework Overview: Interfaces (Collection, List, Set, Queue, Map)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Java Collections Framework provides a unified architecture for representing and manipulating collections. A collection is a group of objects, treated as a single unit. The framework includes interfaces, abstract classes, and concrete implementations, all designed to make working with collections easier and more efficient. This tutorial focuses on the core interfaces of the Collections Framework: `Collection`, `List`, `Set`, `Queue`, and `Map`."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"The Collection Interface"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Collection` interface is the root interface in the collections hierarchy. It represents a group of objects, known as its elements. It defines the basic operations that all collections should support, such as adding elements, removing elements, checking if the collection is empty, and iterating over the elements."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Collection;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class CollectionExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Collection<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

        // Adding elements
        names.add("Alice");
        names.add("Bob");
        names.add("Charlie");

        // Checking the size
        System.out.println("Size: " + names.size()); // Output: Size: 3

        // Checking if empty
        System.out.println("Is empty: " + names.isEmpty()); // Output: Is empty: false

        // Checking if an element exists
        System.out.println("Contains Alice: " + names.contains("Alice")); // Output: Contains Alice: true

        // Removing an element
        names.remove("Bob");

        // Iterating over the elements
        System.out.println("Elements:");
        for (String name : names) {
            System.out.println(name);
        }

        // Clearing the collection
        names.clear();

        System.out.println("Size after clearing: " + names.size()); // Output: Size after clearing: 0
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"The List Interface"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `List` interface is a subinterface of `Collection` that represents an ordered collection of elements. Elements can be accessed by their position (index) in the list. Lists allow duplicate elements."},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Common implementations of the `List` interface include `ArrayList` and `LinkedList`. `ArrayList` is generally more efficient for random access, while `LinkedList` is more efficient for inserting and deleting elements in the middle of the list."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class ListExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> colors = new ArrayList<>();

        // Adding elements
        colors.add("Red");
        colors.add("Green");
        colors.add("Blue");
        colors.add("Red"); // Duplicate element

        // Accessing elements by index
        System.out.println("Element at index 0: " + colors.get(0)); // Output: Element at index 0: Red

        // Inserting an element at a specific index
        colors.add(1, "Yellow");

        // Iterating over the elements
        System.out.println("Elements:");
        for (int i = 0; i < colors.size(); i++) {
            System.out.println("Index " + i + ": " + colors.get(i));
        }

        // Removing an element by index
        colors.remove(2);

        System.out.println("Elements after removal:");
        for (String color : colors) {
            System.out.println(color);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"heading","text":"The Set Interface"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Set` interface is a subinterface of `Collection` that represents a collection of unique elements. Sets do not allow duplicate elements. The `Set` interface models the mathematical set abstraction."},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Common implementations of the `Set` interface include `HashSet`, `LinkedHashSet`, and `TreeSet`. `HashSet` provides the best performance but does not guarantee the order of elements. `LinkedHashSet` maintains the insertion order of elements. `TreeSet` stores elements in a sorted order."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Set;
import java.util.HashSet;

public class SetExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<String> uniqueNames = new HashSet<>();

        // Adding elements
        uniqueNames.add("Alice");
        uniqueNames.add("Bob");
        uniqueNames.add("Charlie");
        uniqueNames.add("Alice"); // Duplicate element - will not be added

        // Checking the size
        System.out.println("Size: " + uniqueNames.size()); // Output: Size: 3

        // Iterating over the elements
        System.out.println("Elements:");
        for (String name : uniqueNames) {
            System.out.println(name);
        }

        // Checking if an element exists
        System.out.println("Contains Alice: " + uniqueNames.contains("Alice")); // Output: Contains Alice: true
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"heading","text":"The Queue Interface"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Queue` interface is a subinterface of `Collection` that represents a collection designed for holding elements prior to processing. Besides basic `Collection` operations, queues provide additional insertion, extraction, and inspection operations. Queues typically, but do not necessarily, order elements in a FIFO (first-in-first-out) manner."},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Common implementations of the `Queue` interface include `LinkedList` and `PriorityQueue`. `LinkedList` can be used as a simple FIFO queue. `PriorityQueue` orders elements according to their priority."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Queue;
import java.util.LinkedList;

public class QueueExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Queue<String> tasks = new LinkedList<>();

        // Adding elements to the queue
        tasks.offer("Task 1");
        tasks.offer("Task 2");
        tasks.offer("Task 3");

        // Peeking at the head of the queue
        System.out.println("Head of the queue: " + tasks.peek()); // Output: Head of the queue: Task 1

        // Removing elements from the queue
        System.out.println("Removing: " + tasks.poll()); // Output: Removing: Task 1
        System.out.println("Removing: " + tasks.poll()); // Output: Removing: Task 2

        // Checking if the queue is empty
        System.out.println("Is empty: " + tasks.isEmpty()); // Output: Is empty: false

        System.out.println("Removing: " + tasks.poll()); // Output: Removing: Task 3
        System.out.println("Is empty: " + tasks.isEmpty()); // Output: Is empty: true
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"heading","text":"The Map Interface"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Map` interface represents a mapping between keys and values. Each key is associated with a value. Maps do not allow duplicate keys; each key can map to at most one value. The `Map` interface is *not* a subinterface of the `Collection` interface."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Common implementations of the `Map` interface include `HashMap`, `LinkedHashMap`, and `TreeMap`. `HashMap` provides the best performance but does not guarantee the order of elements. `LinkedHashMap` maintains the insertion order of keys. `TreeMap` stores keys in a sorted order."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Map;
import java.util.HashMap;

public class MapExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> ages = new HashMap<>();

        // Adding key-value pairs
        ages.put("Alice", 30);
        ages.put("Bob", 25);
        ages.put("Charlie", 35);

        // Getting a value by key
        System.out.println("Alice's age: " + ages.get("Alice")); // Output: Alice's age: 30

        // Checking if a key exists
        System.out.println("Contains key Alice: " + ages.containsKey("Alice")); // Output: Contains key Alice: true

        // Checking if a value exists
        System.out.println("Contains value 25: " + ages.containsValue(25)); // Output: Contains value 25: true

        // Iterating over the key-value pairs
        System.out.println("Key-value pairs:");
        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : ages.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println("Key: " + entry.getKey() + ", Value: " + entry.getValue());
        }

        // Removing a key-value pair
        ages.remove("Bob");

        System.out.println("Key-value pairs after removal:");
        for (String key : ages.keySet()) {
            System.out.println("Key: " + key + ", Value: " + ages.get(key));
        }
    }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In summary, the Java Collections Framework provides a powerful and flexible way to work with collections of objects. Understanding the core interfaces – `Collection`, `List`, `Set`, `Queue`, and `Map` – is essential for effectively using the framework."}]
